# C# Production Conventions (LAP)

Apply these rules for production code. If file is a test file, prioritize test rules.

## 1) File-scoped namespaces

- Prefer **file-scoped** namespaces: `namespace My.Namespace;` then top-level types at root indentation.
- Do **not** wrap the whole file in `namespace X { ... }` unless required for disambiguation or legacy constraints.

```csharp
// Correct
namespace LAP.MyFeature.Abstraction;

public interface IMyService
{
}

// Avoid for new/edited files
namespace LAP.MyFeature.Abstraction
{
    public interface IMyService
    {
    }
}
```

- After `namespace ...;`, add **one blank line** before the first type in the file (`using` directives stay above the namespace).

## 2) Null checks

### Methods

- Use `ArgumentNullException.ThrowIfNull(...)` for required non-string params.
- Use `ArgumentNullException.ThrowIfNullOrWhiteSpace(...)` for required string params.

```csharp
public async Task<Order> CreateOrder(Order order, List<Guid> selectedGuids)
{
    ArgumentNullException.ThrowIfNull(order);
    ArgumentNullException.ThrowIfNull(selectedGuids);
    // ...
}

public void Apply(ITabService tabService, string iconMode)
{
    ArgumentNullException.ThrowIfNull(tabService);
    ArgumentNullException.ThrowIfNullOrWhiteSpace(iconMode);
    // ...
}
```

### Constructors

- Use null-coalescing throw assignment in constructors.
- Do NOT use `ThrowIfNull` directly in constructor body.

```csharp
public sealed class UserService : IUserService
{
    private readonly IUserRepository _userRepository;
    private readonly IEmailService _emailService;

    public UserService(IUserRepository userRepository, IEmailService emailService)
    {
        _userRepository = userRepository ?? throw new ArgumentNullException(nameof(userRepository));
        _emailService = emailService ?? throw new ArgumentNullException(nameof(emailService));
    }
}
```

```csharp
// Wrong in constructor
public UserService(IUserRepository userRepository)
{
    ArgumentNullException.ThrowIfNull(userRepository);
    _userRepository = userRepository;
}
```

## 3) Naming injected dependencies

- Constructor parameter: camelCase from interface name without `I`.
- Field: single underscore + same camelCase root.
- Field and parameter must align one-to-one.

```csharp
public sealed class OrderService : IOrderService
{
    private readonly IOrderRepository _orderRepository;
    private readonly IPaymentGateway _paymentGateway;

    public OrderService(IOrderRepository orderRepository, IPaymentGateway paymentGateway)
    {
        _orderRepository = orderRepository ?? throw new ArgumentNullException(nameof(orderRepository));
        _paymentGateway = paymentGateway ?? throw new ArgumentNullException(nameof(paymentGateway));
    }
}
```

Avoid names like:
- `repository`, `svc`, `repo`, `mockRepo`
- field without underscore (e.g. `orderRepository`)

## 4) Use `sealed` by default for concrete/final types

- Concrete services and concrete records should be `sealed`.
- Do not seal abstract bases.
- **DTOs** must be `sealed record` (not `class`) — see section 17.

```csharp
public sealed class OrderService : IOrderService { }
public sealed record UserDto(int Id, string Name, string Email);

public abstract record BaseEntity(Guid Id, DateTime CreatedAt);
public sealed record User(Guid Id, DateTime CreatedAt, string Name) : BaseEntity(Id, CreatedAt);
```

```csharp
// Wrong when type is not intended for inheritance
public class OrderService : IOrderService { }
public record ProductDto(int Id, string Name);
```

## 5) Object initializer style

- Use `new Type { ... }`
- Do not use `new Type() { ... }`

```csharp
// Correct
var entity = new ClassEntity
{
    Name = "sample"
};

// Wrong
var entity2 = new ClassEntity()
{
    Name = "sample"
};
```

## 6) Dependency registration extension conventions

- Folder: `Extensions`
- File: `DependencyInjectionExtensions.cs`
- Class: `public static class DependencyInjectionExtensions`
- Method shape: `public static void Add...Dependencies(this IServiceCollection services)`

```csharp
public static class DependencyInjectionExtensions
{
    public static void AddAutomatedActionsDependencies(this IServiceCollection services)
    {
        // service registrations
    }
}
```

```csharp
// Wrong naming + wrong return type
public static class ServiceCollectionExtensions
{
    public static IServiceCollection RegisterAutomatedActions(this IServiceCollection services)
    {
        return services;
    }
}
```

## 7) Service interface location

- Interfaces must be directly under `Abstraction`.
- Do not use `Abstractions`, `Contracts`, or nested folders under `Abstraction`.

```text
AutomatedActions/
  Abstraction/
    IAutomatedAction.cs
    IAutomatedActionsManager.cs
    IAutoApplyProfilePhotoActionRunner.cs
    IEnabledAutomatedActionsProvider.cs
```

```text
// Wrong
AutomatedActions/Abstractions/IAutomatedAction.cs
AutomatedActions/Abstraction/Actions/IAutomatedAction.cs
```

## 8) Static code and testability

- Do **not** implement injectable application logic (parsers, adaptors, facades, orchestrators, data access) as `public static class` with static methods. Static collaborators are hard to mock or replace in unit and integration tests.
- Prefer an **`interface` under `Abstraction/`** plus a **`sealed` concrete type** registered in DI (`AddTransient`, `AddScoped`, or `AddSingleton` as appropriate), then inject the interface into packages/services.
- **Never inject concrete implementation types** into constructors, packages, actions, or managers. Always depend on the matching **`I…` interface** from `Abstraction/`; register `IThing` → `Thing` (or open generic `typeof(IThing<,>)` → `typeof(Thing<,>)`) in `DependencyInjectionExtensions`. Unit tests may still `new ConcreteType(...)` when testing that implementation directly.
- **Exceptions** (static is acceptable):
  - `DependencyInjectionExtensions` and similar `Add...Dependencies` extension classes (see section 6).
  - Pure constant holders with **no** test-substitution needs (only `const` / `static readonly` data, no behavior).
  - Framework entry points (e.g. `Program`) and generated/analyzer glue where static is required.

```csharp
// Correct — thin action injects interface only; runner owns view-specific collaborators
public AutoApplyGraphProfilePhoto(
    IAutoApplyProfilePhotoActionRunner<GraphNode, GraphStruct> autoApplyProfilePhotoActionRunner)

// Correct — shared runner injects its own dependencies via closed generic DI
public AutoApplyProfilePhotoActionRunner(
    IOptions<EnableAutoApplyProfilePhotoConfig> applyProfilePhotoPluginsConfig,
    IEffectiveNodeSelectionProvider<GraphNode> effectiveNodeSelectionProvider,
    IProfilePhotosLoader<GraphNode, GraphStruct> profilePhotosLoader,
    IProfilePhotosApplier<GraphNode, GraphStruct> profilePhotosApplier)

// Wrong — concrete type in constructor
public AutoApplyGraphProfilePhoto(
    AutoApplyProfilePhotoActionRunner<GraphNode, GraphStruct> actionRunner)
```

## 9) Service naming convention

- Do **not** use "Service" suffix in concrete service class names.
- Use descriptive noun/verb form: `GroupAttributeParser`, `InstanceAttributeReader`, `AggregationBucketBuilder`.

```csharp
// Correct
public sealed class GroupAttributeParser : IGroupAttributeParser { }

// Wrong
public sealed class GroupAttributeParserService : IGroupAttributeParser { }
```

## 10) Return directly, avoid intermediate variable

- Return the expression directly without storing in an intermediate variable.

```csharp
// Correct
return instances
    .GroupBy(i => _normalizer.Normalize(_attributeReader.GetDisplayValue(i, attributeGuid)))
    .Select(g => new ExploreAggregationGroupItem { Key = g.Key, DocCount = g.Count() })
    .OrderBy(b => b.Key, StringComparer.Ordinal)
    .ToList();

// Wrong
var list = instances
    .GroupBy(i => _normalizer.Normalize(_attributeReader.GetDisplayValue(i, attributeGuid)))
    .Select(g => new ExploreAggregationGroupItem { Key = g.Key, DocCount = g.Count() })
    .OrderBy(b => b.Key, StringComparer.Ordinal)
    .ToList();

return list;
```

## 11) Invalid Guid parsing behavior

- When parsing Guid values from request/input fields (for example group-by fields), do **not** silently return null/false for invalid values in executable flow.
- Throw an explicit argument/domain exception with a clear message and parameter name so upstream callers can fail fast.

## 12) CancellationToken

- For **new** public methods and interfaces that perform I/O, remote calls, scans, or other potentially slow work: add **`CancellationToken cancellationToken = default`** as the **last** parameter so existing callers keep compiling; prefer omitting the argument at call sites when no cancellation is wired yet.
- **Thread** the same token through every layer that participates in the operation (orchestrators, loaders, builders, repositories) and pass it into framework APIs that accept `CancellationToken` (e.g. Elasticsearch client, `Task` overloads).
- Call **`cancellationToken.ThrowIfCancellationRequested()`** only **before** expensive steps (heavy queries, large materialization, pagination over non-`IList`), not on every line. Avoid throwing immediately after trivial work unless the contract requires it.
- **Visualizer plugin `executeFunction` methods** (and any other public entry where reflection matches FE args 1:1): do **not** add `CancellationToken` to the method signature — `BasePluginRequestManager` requires `args.Length == GetParameters().Length`. Prefer a parameter token when the API allows it; when it does not, use **`MethodContext.Context.CancellationToken`** (`Mohaymen.Mrpc.Host.ApiControllerManager`) and call **`MethodContext.Context.CancellationToken.ThrowIfCancellationRequested()`** before expensive steps (same timing rules as above). Pass that token into nested APIs that accept `CancellationToken` when available.

```csharp
public ResponseDto Load(LoadRequest request, CancellationToken cancellationToken = default)
{
    ArgumentNullException.ThrowIfNull(request);

    var rows = _repository.Query(request, cancellationToken);
    cancellationToken.ThrowIfCancellationRequested();
    return _mapper.ToResponse(rows);
}
```

```csharp
// executeFunction-compatible package method (FE sends only business args)
public FileBrowserListFilesResult ListFiles(int from, int size)
{
    MethodContext.Context.CancellationToken.ThrowIfCancellationRequested();
    var documents = TabService.DataService.GetInstances(fileElementId);
    MethodContext.Context.CancellationToken.ThrowIfCancellationRequested();
    return _provider.Provide(documents, from, size);
}
```

## 13) Distributed tracing (OpenTelemetry / Jaeger)

- Inject **`ITraceInstrumentation`** from **`LAP.MicroService.IAPService.Commons.Abstractions`** (host registers `TraceInstrumentation` as a singleton).
- Field **`private readonly ITraceInstrumentation _traceInstrumentation;`**, constructor: **`_traceInstrumentation = traceInstrumentation ?? throw new ArgumentNullException(nameof(traceInstrumentation));`**
- At the **start** of each **meaningful** public or internal entry point (orchestration, loaders, HTTP/package handlers, heavy business methods), start a span and dispose it with the method scope:

```csharp
public sealed class InstanceValuesLoadCoordinator : IInstanceValuesLoadCoordinator
{
    private readonly ITraceInstrumentation _traceInstrumentation;

    public ResponseBaseInstanceParameters Load(..., CancellationToken cancellationToken = default)
    {
        using var activity = _traceInstrumentation.ActivitySource.StartActivity("Coordinate dataset instance values load");
        // ... work; child spans nest automatically when downstream types also StartActivity
    }
}
```

- Use a **literal string** that describes the **business operation** (e.g. `"Load explore view with server-side grouping"`), not class or method names, so spans stay searchable after refactors. Do **not** use `nameof`, variables, or string concatenation for the activity name.
- Nested `StartActivity` calls automatically parent under **`Activity.Current`** when tracing is enabled; use judgment so high-volume inner loops do not emit a span per iteration (span at batch/coordinator level instead).

## 14) Business and applicability validation (FluentValidation)

Separate **null/required guards** (section 2) from **business rules** that decide whether an operation should run.

| Kind | Use | Examples |
|------|-----|----------|
| Required non-null inputs | `ArgumentNullException.ThrowIfNull` / `ThrowIfNullOrWhiteSpace` | `ThrowIfNull(requestParameters)` at method entry |
| Business / applicability rules | **FluentValidation** `AbstractValidator<T>` | non-empty lists, cross-field counts, drill tier, feature preconditions |

When validation is **more than null checks** — empty collections, mismatched list lengths, state/tier/depth, “should this code path run?” — do **not** use:

- private **`static bool Is…Applicable(...)`** helpers
- dedicated **`I…Checker`** types whose only job is duplicating those rules

Use FluentValidation instead (same pattern as [`SearchRequestValidator`](MicroService.IAP/MicroService.IAP/MicroService.IAP/Facade/Validators/Search/SearchRequestValidator.cs) and [`AggregationValuesRequestValidator`](MicroService.IAP/MicroService.IAP/MicroService.IAP/MicroService.IAP.Plugin.DatasetManagement/Validators/AggregationValuesRequestValidator.cs)).

### Validator shape

- Folder: **`Validators/`** at feature/plugin root (e.g. `MicroService.IAP.Plugin.DatasetManagement/Validators/`).
- Class: **`public sealed class …Validator : AbstractValidator<T>`**.
- One **`public const string …ErrorCode`** per rule; attach with **`.WithErrorCode(...)`** only.
- For **soft-fail / applicability** validators (consumer checks `IsValid` and returns null or skips): **do not** add **`.WithMessage(...)`** — error codes are enough for tests and diagnostics.
- For **user-facing hard-fail** (API/package throws to client), localized messages are allowed (e.g. `ExploreSettingsValidator` with `ILanguageDictionaryProvider`).
- Compose rules with **`RuleFor`**, **`Must`**, **`When`** as needed.

```csharp
public sealed class AggregationValuesRequestValidator : AbstractValidator<DatasetRequestParameters>
{
    public const string GroupByFieldsRequiredErrorCode = "GROUP_BY_FIELDS_REQUIRED";

    public AggregationValuesRequestValidator()
    {
        RuleFor(request => request.GroupByFields)
            .NotNull()
            .NotEmpty()
            .WithErrorCode(GroupByFieldsRequiredErrorCode);

        RuleFor(request => request)
            .Must(request => (request.GroupKeys?.Count ?? 0) < request.GroupByFields.Count)
            .When(request => request.GroupByFields is { Count: > 0 })
            .WithErrorCode(AggregationTierRequiredErrorCode);
    }
}
```

### Consumer wiring

- Inject **`IValidator<T>`** (constructor: `validator ?? throw …`).
- Still **`ThrowIfNull`** the request object when the method contract requires it, **before** `Validate`.
- **Soft-fail** (legacy “return null / skip”): `_validator.Validate(request).IsValid` → early exit without throwing.
- **Hard-fail** (API / package boundary): validate and throw or map `ValidationResult` to a domain error — do not re-implement the same rules inline.

```csharp
ArgumentNullException.ThrowIfNull(requestParameters);

if (!_aggregationValuesRequestValidator.Validate(requestParameters).IsValid)
{
    return null;
}
```

### DI

Validators in the **LAP** assembly (`LAP.csproj`) are registered in bulk in [`LapDIContainer.cs`](MicroService.IAP/MicroService.IAP/MicroService.IAP/MicroService.IAPService/LapDIContainer.cs):

```csharp
serviceCollection.AddValidatorsFromAssembly(Assembly.GetExecutingAssembly(), includeInternalTypes: true);
```

- **Do not** duplicate `AddSingleton<IValidator<T>, …>()` / `AddScoped<…>()` in feature `Add…Services` unless you need a **non-default lifetime** (example: [`PrintResultValidator`](MicroService.IAP/MicroService.IAP/MicroService.IAP/Facade/DI/ServiceCollectionExtensions.cs) as **Scoped**).
- SearchForm, PortalSettings, Visualizer, DatasetManagement, Facade search validators, etc. rely on **`AddValidatorsFromAssembly`** — no per-validator registration in their DI extensions.
- Consumers inject **`IValidator<T>`** and resolve it from the container (or `GetRequiredService<IValidator<T>>()` in packages).

## 15) Exception messages (no interpolated values)

- **`throw` message text must be a fixed string literal** inlined at the throw site (or the same literal repeated when multiple sites need identical text). Do **not** embed runtime values in the exception message (`$"…{id}…"`, string concatenation with variables, etc.). Prefer inline literals over extra `const` fields used only once.
- Rationale: stable text for log search, alerting, and documentation; dynamic detail belongs in structured logging, inner exceptions, or dedicated properties — not in `Exception.Message`.
- `ArgumentException` / `ArgumentNullException` may still use `paramName` and, where the BCL supports it, `nameof(...)` for the parameter name only — not free-form interpolated sentences.

```csharp
// Correct
throw new InvalidOperationException(
    "No value aggregate strategy registered for the requested aggregate function.");

// Wrong
throw new InvalidOperationException($"No strategy for function '{aggregateFunction}'.");
```

## 16) Return transformed data; do not mutate caller-owned inputs

- For **new** injectable helpers, mappers, nesters, and similar collaborators: **do not** expose `void` methods whose main job is to **mutate parameters passed in by the caller** (collections, DTOs, response models).
- **Return** the transformed result instead; the **caller** decides whether to assign, replace, or merge it.
- Prefer **`IReadOnlyList<T>`** / **`IReadOnlyCollection<T>`** (or other read-only input shapes) for inputs when the method produces a new output collection.
- **Exceptions** (in-place mutation is acceptable):
  - Explicit builder/accumulator types whose contract is mutation (e.g. `StringBuilder`, streams, ORM trackers).
  - Framework-required patterns (e.g. `Configure`, `Map` onto an existing entity when the API contract is update-in-place).
  - Performance-critical hot paths where allocation must be avoided **and** the in-place contract is documented — prefer this only when necessary, not by default.

```csharp
// Correct — return nested groups; caller assigns
List<ExploreAggregationGroupItem> NestValues(
    IReadOnlyList<ExploreAggregationGroupItem> groups,
    Dictionary<string, List<ExploreAggregationValueRow>> valuesByGroupKey);

response.AggregationGroups = _aggregationValueNester.NestValues(
    response.AggregationGroups,
    aggregationValues);
```

```csharp
// Wrong — mutates caller-owned list with no return value
void NestValues(
    List<ExploreAggregationGroupItem> groups,
    Dictionary<string, List<ExploreAggregationValueRow>> valuesByGroupKey);

_aggregationValueNester.NestValues(response.AggregationGroups, aggregationValues);
```

## 17) DTOs must be `sealed record`

- Request/response models, transfer objects, and similar data carriers (`*Dto`, `*Request`, `*Response`, plugin/API payloads, etc.) **must** be `public sealed record` — not `class`, and not an unsealed `record`.
- Prefer a **primary constructor** when all properties are set at construction; use property-bodied `sealed record` only when defaults / object-initializer / deserializer shape require it.
- Prefer `init` (or primary-constructor parameters) over mutable `set` unless a deserializer or legacy boundary requires setters.
- Do **not** use `class` for new DTOs; when touching an existing DTO `class`, convert it to `sealed record` in the same change when practical.

```csharp
// Correct — primary constructor
public sealed record EntityTagDto(Guid Id, string Title);

// Correct — property-bodied when defaults / initializer shape are needed
public sealed record ApplyTagsRequest
{
    public List<string> InstanceIds { get; init; } = new();
    public List<Guid> AddedTagIds { get; init; } = new();
    public List<Guid> DeletedTagIds { get; init; } = new();
}
```

```csharp
// Wrong
public class ApplyTagsRequest { }
public sealed class EntityTagDto { }
public record EntityTagDto(Guid Id, string Title); // missing sealed
```

Exceptions: framework-required mutable types, generated code, and types that are not DTOs (services, validators, packages).

## 18) DTO records: prefer simple init properties; callers fill them

For DTO / model `sealed record` types, prefer a **simple property-bodied record** with `init` properties. **Callers** construct and populate the values (object initializer). Do **not** require static factories such as `FullTab()` / `Restricted(...)`, `WithoutX()` / `FromX(...)`, or a primary constructor plus one factory per mode.

```csharp
// Correct — simple record; caller fills fields
public sealed record EffectiveMembershipScope
{
    public bool IsFullTabDataset { get; init; }
    public IReadOnlyList<Guid> InstanceIds { get; init; } = Array.Empty<Guid>();
}

return new EffectiveMembershipScope { IsFullTabDataset = true };

return new EffectiveMembershipScope
{
    IsFullTabDataset = false,
    InstanceIds = instanceIds,
};
```

```csharp
// Avoid for new DTOs — mode factories / forced two-mode construction API
public sealed record EffectiveMembershipScope(bool IsFullTabDataset, IReadOnlyList<Guid> InstanceIds)
{
    public static EffectiveMembershipScope FullTab() => new(true, Array.Empty<Guid>());
    public static EffectiveMembershipScope Restricted(IReadOnlyList<Guid> instanceIds) => new(false, instanceIds);
}
```

- Optional computed helpers on the record (e.g. convenience flags derived from the filled fields) are fine when they do not replace an explicit stored discriminator the caller must set.
- Existing types that already use factory methods may remain until touched; when editing them for other reasons, prefer migrating toward the simple init style above.
- Primary-constructor `sealed record` is still OK when all properties are set at construction and there is **no** parallel static factory API for modes.

## 19) Prefer `Provide` over `Build` for factory-style helpers

- When a method (or private helper) **assembles and returns** a domain object / DTO / response from inputs, name it with **`Provide…`**, not **`Build…`**.
- Prefer the same verb on related types already named `…Provider` (`IExploreHistogramProvider`, `ProvideVisualizerHistogram`).
- **Exceptions** (keep `Build` / `Builder`):
  - Framework / BCL / generated APIs (`BuildServiceProvider`, EF `BuildModel`, …).
  - Established GoF-style **Builder** types whose public contract is already `Build…` and renaming would break callers.
  - Third-party or legacy names you must match for wire/protocol compatibility.

```csharp
// Correct
private static VisualizerHistogram ProvideVisualizerHistogram(
    ITabService tabService,
    HashSet<Guid> instanceGuids)

// Wrong
private static VisualizerHistogram BuildVisualizerHistogram(
    ITabService tabService,
    HashSet<Guid> instanceGuids)
```

## 20) Quick checklist

- File-scoped `namespace ...;` for new/edited `.cs` files
- Methods null check: `ThrowIfNull`, `ThrowIfNullOrWhiteSpace`
- Constructor null check: `param ?? throw ...`
- Constructor dependency naming: camelCase from type
- Injected field naming: `_camelCase`
- Final concrete class/record: `sealed`
- **DTOs**: `public sealed record` (not `class`) — section 17
- Object initializer: `new Type { ... }`
- DI extension: `Extensions/DependencyInjectionExtensions.cs` + `Add...Dependencies(...)`
- Interfaces under `Abstraction` root only
- Prefer interface + `sealed` implementation over static classes for testable logic (section 8)
- **DI consumers inject `I…` only** — never concrete implementation types (section 8)
- Service class naming: no "Service" suffix (section 9)
- Return directly, no intermediate variable (section 10)
- Invalid Guid parsing in active flow throws explicit exception (section 11)
- **CancellationToken**: last param `= default` when allowed; otherwise `MethodContext.Context.CancellationToken`; `ThrowIfCancellationRequested` before slow work only (section 12)
- **Tracing**: inject `ITraceInstrumentation`, `using var activity = _traceInstrumentation.ActivitySource.StartActivity(...)` at operation entry (section 13)
- **Business/applicability validation**: FluentValidation `AbstractValidator<T>` + `IValidator<T>` + error codes; not static `IsApplicable` / checker services (section 14)
- **Exception messages**: fixed literals only — no interpolated variables in `throw` message text (section 15)
- **No in-place mutation of caller inputs**: return transformed data; caller assigns or merges (section 16)
- **DTO records**: simple `init` properties; callers fill via object initializer — no required `FullTab`/`Restricted`-style mode factories (section 18)
- **Factory helpers**: `Provide…`, not `Build…` (section 19)
